---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import Docs from '../layouts/Docs.astro';
import Demo from '../components/Demo.astro';
import prefix from '../demos/prefix.html?raw';
import suffix from '../demos/suffix.html?raw';
import rtl from '../demos/rtl.html?raw';
import box from '../demos/box.css?raw';
import react from '../demos/react.tsx?raw';

const themes = { light: 'github-light', dark: 'github-dark' } as const;
---

<Docs title="Getting started">
  <h2>Install</h2>
  <Code code="npm install sabi-input" lang="bash" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} />

  <h2>Add it</h2>
  <p>Import it once, anywhere in your app. That registers <code>&lt;sabi-input&gt;</code> and adds its styles. Then wrap any text <code>&lt;input&gt;</code> with it.</p>
  <Code code="import 'sabi-input';" lang="js" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} />
  <Demo code={prefix} />
  <p>The input stays yours: its value, its events and form submission work as they would without Sabi.</p>

  <h2>Style the box on a parent</h2>
  <p><code>&lt;sabi-input&gt;</code> is the strip of text that scrolls, not the field around it. The fade is a mask, and a mask fades everything the element paints, so a background or border on the element itself would fade too. Put those on a parent, along with the focus style. Vertical padding goes on <code>&lt;sabi-input&gt;</code> itself, so the strip fills the box. It takes the box's rounded corners automatically, so anything it clips, like iPhone selection handles, is cut to the box's shape.</p>
  <Code code={box.trim()} lang="css" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} />

  <h2>Prefix and suffix</h2>
  <p>An element before the input is a prefix. Clicking it focuses the input. An element after the input follows the end of the text as you type. Both are dimmed.</p>
  <Demo code={suffix} />

  <h2>Right-to-left</h2>
  <p>Sabi follows the field's direction: set <code>dir="rtl"</code> on <code>&lt;sabi-input&gt;</code> or any parent, and the text starts on the right, the prefix sits on the right and the suffix follows the end of the text on the left. For text whose direction you don't know in advance, put <code>dir="auto"</code> on the input: the field then takes the direction of whatever is typed. Text that mixes directions, like Hebrew with numbers or English names, keeps its caret clear of the fades too.</p>
  <Demo code={rtl} />
  <p>Sabi never moves the caret. Arrow keys belong to the browser, so in mixed Hebrew and English text the caret moves the way it does in any other field in that browser: by screen side in Safari and Firefox, and in reading order in Chrome and Edge, where it jumps to the far end of an English word and walks back through it. Sabi keeps it in view either way.</p>

  <h2>React</h2>
  <p>Works with controlled inputs. In TypeScript, the <code>sabi-input/react</code> import lets <code>&lt;sabi-input&gt;</code> type-check in JSX; add it once.</p>
  <Code code={react.trim()} lang="tsx" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} />
  <p>Importing on the server, as Next.js and other server renderers do, is safe: the element is registered in the browser.</p>
</Docs>
